<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert III Achilles, Elector of Brandenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1414">1414</a>–<a href="/years/1486">1486</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Albrecht Achilles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert III <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> was a Hohenzollern prince, Margrave of Ansbach from 1440 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a> from 1464, and Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> from 1470. A renowned soldier and tournament fighter, he fought long wars against the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and other neighbours. In 1473 he issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dispositio-achillea">Dispositio Achillea</a>, which ruled that Brandenburg should pass undivided to the eldest son while the Franconian lands went to younger sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-iii-achilles-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Albert III Achilles, Elector of Brandenburg" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markus Frankl · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albrecht_Achilles_auf_der_Predella_des_von_ihm_gestifteten_Schwanenordensaltars_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
